#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051068 :topic/id #uuid "7e482952-bd90-56e1-a587-ef0d76b7d965" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166626 :question/id #uuid "aeb0ab38-6a30-586d-bcb4-8d3c6b8c9d9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8409" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} + y + 6 = y^2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-3$\n\n- $y=-2$\n\n- $y=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(t) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} = y^2 - y - 6,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y) = y^2 - y - 6.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0.$  $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0.$$\\quad  \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0.$ $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166627 :question/id #uuid "ccdc8d9f-641a-593f-8999-ea02e14b3ed3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8410" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^2+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)= y^2+4y,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}+4c & =0 \\\\ c(c+4) & =0 \\\\ c & =-4,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_1(x)=-4$ and $y_2(x)=0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166628 :question/id #uuid "eaeec2ed-cb61-5887-94a8-b7ab30aa5d1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8411" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x)=4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and $y_3(x)$\n\n- $y_1(x) + y_2(x)+y_3(x) =-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(4)=4^3-3(4)^2-6(4)+8 = 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=4$ is a solution to $f(y) = 0,$ and so $(y-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\">$y^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$y^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$y^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$y^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\">$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-4)({y}^{2}+y-2) \\\\  & =(y-4)(y-1)(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\ny_1(x) =4, \\quad y_2(x) = 1, \\quad y_3(x) = -2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have $y_1(x)+y_2(x) + y_3(x) = 4+1+(-2) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3184 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050011}] :topic/title "Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs"}]}
